# Astro's build output. Unlike the `app/` tree this replaced, it is NOT committed: it is
# reproducible from `src/` with `pnpm build`, and a hashed bundle in history is churn.
#
# Anchored with a leading slash, and that matters: a bare `dist/` has no slash before its last
# character, so git matches it at every depth. `tests/dist/` — the suite that reports on the build
# — was invisible in `git status` on the commit that added it, which is the same shape of defect as
# the `.vscode/` note at the foot of the repository root's `.gitignore`. There is one build output
# here and it is `web/dist/`; say so.
/dist/
.astro/

# `tests/dist/` is committed, and this negation is what makes it visible.
#
# Anchoring the rule above was only half of it: `dist` — no slash, so every depth again — is also in
# a personal `~/.gitignore_global` on at least one machine here, and a global exclude file cannot be
# fixed from inside the repository. The same mechanism as `!.vscode/` at the foot of the repository
# root's `.gitignore`, for the same reason: a rule of one developer's own must not decide what is in
# everyone's checkout. A repository `.gitignore` outranks `core.excludesFile`, so this re-includes
# it for everyone.
!tests/dist/

node_modules/

# Playwright
test-results/
playwright-report/
blob-report/
.playwright/

# The visual baseline (`scripts/baseline.ts`). Local by design and not committed: a screenshot is a
# rendering of text, this site ships no font binary, and the platform faces disagree — so a
# committed reference encodes one machine's fonts as the truth and is red everywhere else. The
# script's header has the long version.
.baseline/

# The measure report, when written with `--json`. Reproducible from a build, and it carries a
# timestamp, so committing one is churn.
measure.json
